==========================================
SYSADMIN DAY 10 NOTES — Capstone: Mini Server Hardening & Audit
Date: 24-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- A sysadmin's loop: what's exposed -> what blocks it -> what stops brute force -> who's watching -> is it housekept
- ss / lsof show what is listening; 0.0.0.0 means reachable from the network, 127.0.0.1 only from this machine
- Reading the firewall (ufw status, iptables -S) is safe; changing a live ufw + Docker firewall is not a casual step
- fail2ban banip with a documentation address (192.0.2.10) gives a real ban without touching localhost's ignore list
- A ban shows up in three places: jail status, the firewall chain, and the fail2ban log - unban cleans both sides
- auditd with auid tells me who REALLY changed a security file, even through sudo
- logrotate and ncdu/df keep the logs from today's tools from quietly filling the disk
- Every tool from Days 01-09 fits one workflow: each answers one question about the machine

COMMANDS / ACTIONS I PRACTICED:
[ ] pre-flight: systemctl is-active, enable --now, tmux session
[ ] ss -tulpn and lsof -i | grep LISTEN
[ ] ufw status verbose and iptables -S (read only)
[ ] fail2ban jail check, manual banip, firewall chain, log line, unbanip
[ ] auditd watch on jail.local, chmod trigger, ausearch -k, watch removed
[ ] logrotate config + dry run for fail2ban, du/df/ncdu on /var/log
[ ] (bonus) copytruncate demo with a running writer
[ ] filled in the hardening scorecard

